[DONE] Once the number of risen in the squad increases a lot, it starts to cut into the visuals of the log and makes it hard to read. We have plenty of vertical space to work with, so maybe it can naturally push the log upwards if there are too many names in the squad.
    `#log` was pinned at a fixed `bottom:150px` while `#squadbar` wraps
    upward from `bottom:10px`, so the two only ever agreed at one host size. The log
    now stands on top of the bar: the bar's real height is measured after every
    rebuild and the log's floor set just above it. Measured rather than computed from
    a row count, because the rows wrap against `max-width` and the true height
    depends on the window. Forty risen make a 320px bar and the log lifts to 342px;
    a small squad never drops it below the old 150. Touch layout is deliberately left
    alone — it pins the log to its own sheet with `!important`.

[DONE] Iron ore should also be sold in the world. Currently no merchant sells them. I would think a place like Ironscar would have that on the market. I actually can’t even seem to find a place to mine for it – does it even exist? Made the endgame impossible to close the door since it required a ton of iron.
    It existed in the GROUND — about 80 vein tiles and three ore fields —
    and nowhere else. Every miner in the world produced copper, every smith SPENT
    copper, and no town's opening stock listed iron ore, coal or bar iron, so the
    only iron that could ever exist was what you dug personally. That is the whole
    reason the door could not be closed by trade.
    Fixed as an economy rather than as a shelf: Ironscar is an open pit and its
    miners bring up iron ore and coal, everyone else turns some up, and smiths smelt
    ore + coal into bar iron before they touch copper. Opening stock seeded to match
    (the pit opens with ~70 ore), and priced by place — x0.45 at the pit, x1.15 at
    Copperhold, x1.35 at Dustport — so where you buy it means something.

